Heat given to water in a boiler goes into three jobs: warming the water, boiling it (latent heat) and superheating the steam. As the pressure rises, the **latent heat becomes smaller** (it is zero at the critical point). Modern utility boilers work at very high pressure and very high final steam temperature, so the **superheating share becomes large: more than 40 %** of the total heat.

**Option logic:** (B), (C) and (D) all say "less than …", and (C) and (D) are already inside (B). If any of them were true, (B) would be true too, giving more than one correct answer. Only (A) stands alone.

**Answer: (A).**
