    VALUES (subj, tid, 'NUMERICAL', 'A steam power station of 100 MW capacity uses coal of calorific value of 6400 kcal/kg. The thermal efficiency of the station is 30% and electrical...', 'A steam power station of 100 MW capacity uses coal of calorific value of 6400 kcal/kg. The thermal efficiency of the station is 30% and electrical generation efficiency is 92%. When the station is working at full load, the coal required per hour will be nearly
', '**Idea used:** coal per hour $= P\times860/(\eta_{overall}\times CV)$ — see *Steam (thermal) power plants → Efficiency, heat rate and coal consumption* (Example 1).

- Overall efficiency $= 0.30\times0.92 = 0.276$.
- Electrical energy in one hour $= 100\,000$ kWh $= 100\,000\times860 = 8.6\times10^7$ kcal.
- Heat needed from coal $= 8.6\times10^7/0.276 \approx 3.116\times10^8$ kcal.
- Coal $= 3.116\times10^8/6400 \approx 48\,690$ kg $\approx 48.7$ tonnes.

**Without a calculator:** $860/6400 = 0.134$; so coal $= \dfrac{100\,000\times0.134}{0.276}$ kg. Now $0.134/0.276 \approx 0.49$ (since $0.276\times0.5 = 0.138$, a little more than 0.134). So about $0.487\times100\,000 \approx 48\,700$ kg.

**Trap:** using only the thermal efficiency (0.30) gives $8.6\times10^7/(0.30\times6400) = 44.8$ t, which is not an option. Always divide by the **overall** efficiency (thermal × generator); the generator loses a little more, so more coal is needed, not less.

**Answer: (B) 48.7 tonnes.**
